,"question":"Let $U=span((1,0))$ and $W=span((2,0))$ in $R^2$. What is $U+W$?","options":["$span((0,1))$","$span((1,0))$","${0}$","$R^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d05007c879e3b50e293c1e18","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch19_sum_of_subspaces_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $U={0}$ and W is a subspace, what is $U+W$?","options":["$U∩W$ only.","$W$","${0}$","$V$ necessarily."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"37d79964b1a010446f80ce34","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch19_sum_of_subspaces_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For subspaces $U,W$ of a vector space, why is every vector of $U$ also in their sum $U+W$?","options":["Every $u∈U$ can be written as $u+0$ with $0∈W$.","The sum is defined as the intersection.","Every vector of U must also lie in W.","This is true only when U and W are equal."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8303a11267d0f6d2bfea23b6","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch19_sum_of_subspaces_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does it mean that $U+W$ is the smallest subspace containing U and W?","options":["$U+W$ is always equal to $U$","$U+W$ contains no vector outside $U∩W$.","There is no subspace containing both U and W.","Any subspace with both $U$ and $W$ must contain $U+W$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b048879475b5ba64cc4cf6ae","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch19_sum_of_subspaces_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $U=span((1,0,0))$ and $W=span((0,1,0))$ in $R^3$. What is $U+W$?","options":["The xy-plane $\\{(x,y,0)\\}$","The z-axis.","The line $span((1,1,1))$.","$R^3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"864f83da9ee62f9fe5761d9b","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch19_sum_of_subspaces_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $dim U=2$, $dim W=2$, and $dim(U∩W)=1$, what is $dim(U+W)$?","options":["$1$","$2$","$3$","$4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2c759d3a2f0b6d8542a2b2c4","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch19_sum_of_subspaces_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $U=span((1,0,0),(0,1,0))$ and $W=span((0,0,1))$. Does $(2,3,4)$ belong to $U+W$?","options":["No, because U and W intersect trivially.","Yes, $(2,3,4)=(2,3,0)+(0,0,4)$.","No, because the vector has three nonzero coordinates.","Only if 4=0."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"028433834da9ad9475db90b9","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch19_sum_of_subspaces_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can a sum of subspaces have non-unique representations?","options":["Because vector addition is commutative, so components can be swapped","A nonzero overlap vector can move from one component to another","Each component can be rescaled without changing the vector it sums to","Subspaces of different dimensions can never meet only at zero"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ed50ada4370ff7678bb53f5b","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch19_sum_of_subspaces_018_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $U=\\operatorname{span}(\\boldsymbol{a},\\boldsymbol{b})$ and $W=\\operatorname{span}(\\boldsymbol{c})$ be subspaces of $V$. Which set equals $U+W$ for every choice of the vectors $\\boldsymbol{a},\\boldsymbol{b},\\boldsymbol{c}$?","options":["$\\operatorname{span}(\\boldsymbol{a},\\boldsymbol{b},\\boldsymbol{c})$","$\\operatorname{span}(\\boldsymbol{a}+\\boldsymbol{c},\\boldsymbol{b}+\\boldsymbol{c})$","$\\operatorname{span}(\\boldsymbol{a}+\\boldsymbol{b}+\\boldsymbol{c})$","$U\\cup W$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f2528fc3b93ab815e085d31c","time_limit":30}]}}